DERIVATION FROM PRIOR WORK
==========================

This program is a derivative work of "Mutt OAuth2 token management script"
(contrib/mutt_oauth2.py in the Mutt source distribution):

    Copyright (C) 2020 Alexander Perlis
    Licensed under the GNU General Public License, either version 2 of the
    License, or (at your option) any later version.
    https://gitlab.com/muttmua/mutt/-/blob/master/contrib/mutt_oauth2.py

omt is a rewrite of that script in Go. It is not a clean-room
reimplementation: the OAuth2 flows, the configuration file format, the
stored-token layout and a number of deliberate behavioural quirks were
carried over so that existing users and existing config files keep working.
Individual functions in this codebase still carry comments noting which part
of the Python original they mirror.

The original grants the work under GPL version 2 "or (at your option) any
later version". This derivative exercises that option and is distributed
under GPL version 3 or later, which is additionally necessary because omt
links against Apache-2.0 licensed dependencies (spf13/cobra and its
transitive dependencies) that are not compatible with GPL version 2.


STATEMENT OF CHANGES
====================

Per section 5(a) of the GNU General Public License version 3, this work
carries prominent notices that it is modified. The substantive changes
relative to mutt_oauth2.py are:

  - Reimplemented in Go rather than Python.
  - Token state moved from a GPG-encrypted file to the operating system
    keyring (macOS Keychain, Windows Credential Manager, Linux Secret
    Service), with transparent chunking for tokens exceeding a backend's
    per-entry size limit.
  - Added subcommands: authorize, refresh (single account or all),
    list-accounts, status, token, daemon, version, completion.
  - Added a long-running daemon that refreshes tokens on its own schedule,
    so tokens do not go stale across machine sleep/wake cycles.
  - Added optional automatic launching of a configured browser (including a
    specific application and profile) for interactive authorization.
  - Added capture of the refresh token's own expiry when a provider reports
    one.
  - Output discipline changed: only the access token (or a reporting
    command's table) is written to stdout; all notices, prompts and warnings
    go to stderr.
  - The refresh token is no longer printed on every authorization or
    refresh; it is now shown only under -v/--verbose.
  - Command-line parsing moved to spf13/cobra, with generated help, man
    pages and shell completions.


